---
import CalculatorLayout from "../../layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro";
import IdealWeightCalculator from "../../components/IdealWeightCalculator.astro";
import FaqAccordion from "../../components/FaqAccordion.astro";

const relatedCalcs = [
  { name: "BMI Calculator",              href: "/bmi-calculator/",              desc: "See where your current weight falls on the BMI scale" },
  { name: "Body Fat % Calculator",       href: "/body-fat-calculator/",         desc: "A more precise measure than weight alone" },
  { name: "TDEE Calculator",             href: "/tdee-calculator/",             desc: "Find your daily calorie needs" },
  { name: "Calorie Deficit Calculator",  href: "/calorie-deficit-calculator/",  desc: "Set a realistic calorie target to reach your goal" },
];

const faqSchema = [
  {
    question: "What is the most accurate ideal weight formula?",
    answer: "No single formula is universally best. The Devine formula (1974) is the most widely cited in clinical settings, particularly for drug dosing calculations. For general health purposes, the BMI healthy weight range (18.5–24.9) applied to your height is more flexible and accounts for natural body weight variation. Athletes and people with high muscle mass should use body fat percentage as a better guide.",
  },
  {
    question: "Is ideal weight the same as healthy weight?",
    answer: "Not exactly. Ideal weight formulas were developed for clinical contexts and give a single-point estimate. Healthy weight is better understood as a range. The BMI 18.5–24.9 range applied to your height gives a span of weights where health risks are generally lowest. Most people's actual ideal weight sits somewhere in that range.",
  },
  {
    question: "Do ideal weight formulas account for muscle mass?",
    answer: "No, they do not. These formulas only use height and sex as inputs. A muscular 5'10\" athlete and a sedentary 5'10\" person would get the same ideal weight number, which is one reason body fat percentage is often a more meaningful target than a weight number.",
  },
  {
    question: "Should I try to reach my ideal weight?",
    answer: "Use ideal weight as a reference point, not a rigid target. Health is better measured by body fat percentage, metabolic markers (blood pressure, blood glucose, cholesterol), and how you feel and perform physically. A healthy weight range, sustained consistently, matters more than hitting a precise number.",
  },
];
---

<CalculatorLayout
  title="Ideal Weight Calculator | No Sign-Up | CaloricLab"
  pageTitle="Ideal Weight Calculator"
  description="Calculate your target weight for your height and frame. Uses science-backed formulas to find your healthiest range. Instant results with no account."
  subtitle="Find your ideal weight range using multiple evidence-based formulas. See what different approaches recommend for your height."
  breadcrumb="Ideal Weight Calculator"
  canonicalUrl="https://caloriclab.com/ideal-weight-calculator/"
  {relatedCalcs}
  {faqSchema}
>
  <IdealWeightCalculator slot="calculator" />

  <div slot="content">
    <div class="prose prose-gray max-w-none">

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">What Is Ideal Body Weight?</h2>
      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Ideal body weight (IBW) is the weight considered optimal for your height based on clinical research. Because bodies vary in muscle mass, bone density, and fat distribution, there's no single correct number — IBW is a reference range, not a precise target. The three formulas on this page were developed by pharmacologists in the 1970s–80s to help calculate safe drug doses, and have since been widely adopted as general health reference points.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">How the Formulas Compare</h2>

      <div class="not-prose bg-white rounded-2xl shadow-sm overflow-hidden mb-6">
        <div class="divide-y divide-gray-100">
          <div class="px-5 py-4" style="background-color: var(--cl-violet-subtle);">
            <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-1">
              <p class="text-sm font-bold" style="color: var(--cl-violet-dark);">Devine (1974)</p>
              <span class="text-xs font-medium bg-white px-2 py-0.5 rounded-full" style="color: var(--cl-violet-dark);">Most widely used</span>
            </div>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-700">The standard in clinical settings for over 50 years. Most fitness references default to this formula.</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 mt-1">Best for: average-height adults as a general baseline</p>
          </div>
          <div class="px-5 py-4">
            <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-1">
              <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-800">Robinson (1983)</p>
            </div>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600">A modest revision to Devine with slightly adjusted base values. Results are similar but diverge at taller heights due to a lower per-inch increment.</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 mt-1">Best for: general estimates across a range of heights</p>
          </div>
          <div class="px-5 py-4">
            <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-1">
              <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-800">Miller (1983)</p>
            </div>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600">Higher starting weight, smaller per-inch increment. Gives higher estimates for shorter people and lower for taller — useful as a second opinion.</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 mt-1">Best for: taller individuals who find Devine too lean</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">Ideal Weight vs. Body Composition</h2>
      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-3">
        Scale weight alone doesn't tell you how much is muscle versus fat. A muscular person may weigh more than these formula estimates and be perfectly healthy — the formulas don't account for body composition at all. For a more complete picture, use the <a href="/body-fat-calculator/" class="text-green-700 hover:text-violet-dark underline underline-offset-2">Body Fat Calculator</a> alongside these estimates.
      </p>
      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        The <a href="/bmi-calculator/" class="text-green-700 hover:text-violet-dark underline underline-offset-2">BMI healthy range</a> (18.5–24.9) gives a broader, more flexible target for most people — a span of 25–40 lbs rather than a single number. If your current weight falls within that range, the IBW formula values are largely academic. If you're outside it, the BMI range is the more actionable target.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">How to Reach Your Ideal Weight</h2>
      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-3">
        <strong class="text-gray-800">If you're above your range:</strong> Start by calculating your <a href="/tdee-calculator/" class="text-green-700 hover:text-violet-dark underline underline-offset-2">TDEE</a> to find your maintenance calories, then use the <a href="/calorie-deficit-calculator/" class="text-green-700 hover:text-violet-dark underline underline-offset-2">Calorie Deficit Calculator</a> to set a realistic daily target. Use the <a href="/protein-calculator/" class="text-green-700 hover:text-violet-dark underline underline-offset-2">Protein Calculator</a> to hit a daily protein target that preserves muscle while you lose fat.
      </p>
      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-3">
        <strong class="text-gray-800">If you're below your range:</strong> Use the <a href="/macro-calculator/" class="text-green-700 hover:text-violet-dark underline underline-offset-2">Macro Calculator</a> to build a slight calorie surplus with adequate protein, and prioritize resistance training to add lean mass rather than just body fat.
      </p>
      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        For a deeper look at how to structure your approach, see <a href="/blog/cutting-vs-bulking-calories/" class="text-green-700 hover:text-violet-dark underline underline-offset-2">Cutting vs. Bulking: How to Know Which One You Actually Need</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <FaqAccordion items={faqSchema} />

    </div>
  </div>
</CalculatorLayout>
